{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1350,
    "dDurationMs": 4429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most physical objects in everyday life maintain\nthe same identity whether we’re interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5779,
    "dDurationMs": 1191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with them, or not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6970,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, a baseball is a baseball whether you're\nholding it in your hand or it's flying through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the air."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12430,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even electrons are electrons whether they're\npart of an atom or flying freely through interstellar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17360,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space; they have the same mass, same charge:\nthey are electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22310,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But neutrinos, those weird, super light, super\nfast, electrically neutral, hard-to-interact-with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27110,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles - they are identity-agnostic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31090,
    "dDurationMs": 3519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrinos have different identities depending\non whether they’re interacting with other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34609,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles, or traveling freely, and on top\nof that their identities can change over time!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39190,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s what I mean: when they interact with\nother particles, like when they're produced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43329,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fusion in the sun or in radioactive decay,\nthere are three different kinds of neutrinos,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47789,
    "dDurationMs": 4281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "characterized by the particles involved in\ntheir creation or annihilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52070,
    "dDurationMs": 3629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when they're traveling through space,\nthere are three different kinds of neutrinos,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55699,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "characterized by their masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57589,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these two sets of identities don't match\nup in a one-to-one correspondence; instead,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62360,
    "dDurationMs": 5689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each of the \"interaction\" identities is actually\na mix of the three \"traveling\" identities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68049,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this weirdness allows neutrinos to change\ntheir identities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71880,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's because the traveling identities have\ndifferent masses, so they travel differently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75490,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from each other – technically what happens\nis they each pick up a complex-numbered phase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79040,
    "dDurationMs": 3289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depending on their mass and how far they’ve\ntraveled, but I’ll just show that using"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82329,
    "dDurationMs": 3171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrows that rotate at different speeds, which\nis essentially the same thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85500,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, because the arrows rotate at different\nspeeds, over time a combination that initially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89729,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looked like an electron-interacting neutrino\nmight become the muon-interacting combination."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94409,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then, if you wait longer, the combination\nwill look like an electron-interacting neutrino"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99820,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again, then muon-interacting, and so on, back\nand forth – all happening as the neutrino"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105060,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flies super fast through space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107150,
    "dDurationMs": 3819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s kind of like if I took my violin and\nplayed an A , and somehow by the time the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110969,
    "dDurationMs": 3961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sound waves reached your ear, the relative\nstrengths of the frequencies had shifted so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114930,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you heard the sound as an E, or a D, or an\nA, depending on how far away you were."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120009,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, the oscillation of neutrinos back\nand forth between different identities was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124200,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovered in part because we didn’t see\nas many neutrinos coming from the sun as our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128220,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of fusion suggested."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130510,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turned out that about 2/3 of the electron-interacting\nneutrinos had turned into muon and tau-interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135680,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos en route to the earth, in a very\nreal, very long-range example of quantum superposition!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142470,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, and one other little technicality: even\nthough the three “interaction”-neutrinos"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147640,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are named after the specific electron-family\nparticles that are involved in their creation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152050,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and annihilation, they can still interact\nwith (that’s physics-speak for “bounce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155190,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off of”) other members of the electron family\nas well as quarks ."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158720,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, I’d like to thank the Heising Simons\nfoundation for their support of this video"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163630,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and of neutrino research!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165480,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They put me in touch with some of the neutrino\nresearchers they help fund, who were awesome"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168760,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to talk to and learn from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170350,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Heising Simons also funds a variety of\nresearch in other fields, like exoplanets,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175200,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microscale gravity, climate change, and so\non."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178650,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Heising Simons, check\nout heisingsimons.org."
    } ]
  } ]
}
